{"name":"Light Breeze","hex":"#cfe0f2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"light breeze","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["On the Beaufort scale, a force-2 wind (4-6 knots)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light_breeze"}],"entries":[{"title":"Light Breeze","blocks":[{"text":"Light Breeze is an album by the flugelhornist and composer Franco Ambrosetti which was recorded in 1997 and released on the Enja label the following year."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/ed/Light_Breeze.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light_Breeze","wikidata":"Q55616851"},{"title":"Beaufort scale","blocks":[{"text":"The Beaufort scale is an empirical measure that relates wind speed to observed conditions at sea or on land. Its full name is the Beaufort wind force scale. It was devised in 1805 by Francis Beaufort, a hydrographer in the British Royal Navy. It was officially adopted by the Royal Navy and later spread internationally."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Beaufort_scale_12_notext.jpg/330px-Beaufort_scale_12_notext.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beaufort_scale","wikidata":"Q170464"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnai